WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 6: Quality, feedback, and observability — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.610
Software engineering 201. Lesson six.

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.720
Quality, feedback, and observability.

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.125
Picture a made-up billing system.

4
00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:11.678
Last night, it renewed its subscriptions.

5
00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.102
This morning the build is green, and every test passed.

6
00:00:15.102 --> 00:00:18.714
Was every customer charged the right amount, exactly once?

7
00:00:18.714 --> 00:00:21.827
Yes, the tests passed? Probably, coverage is high?

8
00:00:21.827 --> 00:00:23.633
Or: you can't tell from that?

9
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:28.054
You can't tell from that. Green says the code did what its tests asked.

10
00:00:28.054 --> 00:00:30.980
It says nothing about what happened last night.

11
00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.034
Back to the lens from lesson one: architecture bends around what's scarce.

12
00:00:36.034 --> 00:00:39.848
Ten years ago, the scarce thing was seeing the whole system.

13
00:00:39.848 --> 00:00:43.154
The answer was observability: logs, metrics, traces.

14
00:00:43.154 --> 00:00:44.870
They answer: what happened?

15
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.922
Now the scarce thing is knowing it's right.

16
00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:49.682
That's a different question.

17
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:54.772
And no dashboard can answer it, until somebody has written down what right means.

18
00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:57.538
So: define right, up front. Watch every run.

19
00:00:57.538 --> 00:00:59.360
And feed back what you learn.

20
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.338
Take our made-up example: the subscription-billing system from lesson one.

21
00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:06.273
Here are last night's renewals.

22
00:01:06.273 --> 00:01:10.142
And the build is green. But no test ever looked at this table.

23
00:01:10.142 --> 00:01:12.950
So was last night right? The green can't say.

24
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.455
So before the work starts, write down what right means, in the business's words.

25
00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:21.871
Every renewal charged the right amount, exactly once.

26
00:01:21.871 --> 00:01:24.770
Then split that into checks an agent can run.

27
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.575
One: none was charged twice.

28
00:01:26.575 --> 00:01:29.217
Two: each charge equals the plan's price.

29
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:32.310
Three: every renewal that was due was attempted.

30
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.493
Now run them, on last night's real renewals.

31
00:01:35.493 --> 00:01:38.391
One was charged twice. One, the wrong amount.

32
00:01:38.391 --> 00:01:42.255
One was never attempted. And look at the totals: they match.

33
00:01:42.255 --> 00:01:45.861
Three wrong renewals, inside a sum that's exactly right.

34
00:01:45.861 --> 00:01:48.630
So check every renewal, not just the total.

35
00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.209
That one sentence does double duty.

36
00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:53.948
You write the check once, and run it twice.

37
00:01:53.948 --> 00:01:57.705
Before a change ships, on sample renewals, in the pipeline.

38
00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:00.635
And after every nightly run, on the real ones.

39
00:02:00.635 --> 00:02:09.933
Add a target for time, too. A service level objective, an SLO: say, ninety-nine point nine percent of renewals due today, attempted by six A.M.

40
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:14.710
Now an agent can run all of it, without asking anyone what good looks like.

41
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.553
The checks say that something is wrong.

42
00:02:17.553 --> 00:02:24.647
The record says why. So every step of every run leaves a line, in a shape that people and agents can both read.

43
00:02:24.647 --> 00:02:27.779
Here's the double charge. Attempt one: a timeout.

44
00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:30.144
Attempt two, with a new key: charged.

45
00:02:30.144 --> 00:02:34.235
Then reconcile, against the processor's own report: two charges.

46
00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:36.472
The first attempt had gone through.

47
00:02:36.472 --> 00:02:37.750
Nobody has to guess.

48
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.715
First, put last night right, with a person approving, as in lesson five.

49
00:02:42.715 --> 00:02:47.394
Then close the loop. Every miss goes back into the brief, and the checks.

50
00:02:47.394 --> 00:02:52.714
The double charge becomes a new line in the brief: a retry must reuse the same key.

51
00:02:52.714 --> 00:02:55.791
That's an idempotency key; more in lesson eight.

52
00:02:55.791 --> 00:02:58.354
And a new test: the processor times out.

53
00:02:58.354 --> 00:03:00.790
So each run makes the next one better.

54
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.865
And be honest about the limit.

55
00:03:02.865 --> 00:03:05.757
A check only catches what somebody thought to ask.

56
00:03:05.757 --> 00:03:08.649
So a person still reads a sample of real invoices.

57
00:03:08.649 --> 00:03:11.310
And whatever they find becomes the next check.

58
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.384
So, to recap. Green doesn't mean right.

59
00:03:14.384 --> 00:03:17.457
Define right first, in the business's words.

60
00:03:17.457 --> 00:03:20.460
Check before you ship, and after every run.

61
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.625
Make every step leave a record.

62
00:03:22.625 --> 00:03:24.580
And feed every miss back in.

63
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.376
Which gives us rule six, for your agents.

64
00:03:27.376 --> 00:03:32.983
Before you build, write down what right means, as checks you can run, in the business's words.

65
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:36.084
Run them before a change ships, and after every run.

66
00:03:36.084 --> 00:03:37.814
Leave a record at every step.

67
00:03:37.814 --> 00:03:40.379
When a check fails, stop and tell a person.

68
00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:43.660
And feed every miss back into the brief and the checks.

69
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.642
Now, your turn. Pick one job your system runs every day.

70
00:03:47.642 --> 00:03:52.376
Ask your AI: what would have to be true for last night's run to be right?

71
00:03:52.376 --> 00:03:56.397
Write it as three read-only checks I can run on the real data.

72
00:03:56.397 --> 00:03:57.240
Then run one.

73
00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:03.790
Once a job has a brief, its checks, and a record of every run, it's ready to be reused.

74
00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.570
That's next: skills as building blocks.

75
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.280
See you in lesson seven.
